<p>One walkthrough video can work in many places: your website, the platforms where you list, YouTube, Instagram, TikTok and even WhatsApp. The trick is using the right format in the right place. Every video from Still to Film comes in two formats, 16:9 and 9:16, so you have what you need for both. Here is where each one goes, and what to check before you upload.</p>

<h2>What is the difference between 16:9 and 9:16?</h2>
<p><strong>16:9</strong> is landscape: wider than it is tall, like a television or a laptop screen. <strong>9:16</strong> is the same shape turned upright, filling a phone screen held vertically. The content is the same walkthrough; the framing is adjusted so each shot works in that shape.</p>

<h2>Where does the 16:9 version go?</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Your website.</strong> As a hero video at the top of the home page, or on the page for a specific yacht, room type or property. On a desktop screen, landscape fills the space naturally.</li>
  <li><strong>Listing and booking platforms that accept video.</strong> Some charter and booking platforms let you add a video to a listing, either as an upload or as a link to YouTube or Vimeo. Others only take photos.</li>
  <li><strong>YouTube.</strong> As a regular video, which you can also embed on your website or link from a listing.</li>
  <li><strong>Presentations and email.</strong> A link to the video in a quote or an offer to a client, or played in a sales meeting.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Where does the 9:16 version go?</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Instagram Reels and Stories.</strong> Vertical video fills the screen, which is how most people watch there.</li>
  <li><strong>TikTok.</strong> Built for vertical video.</li>
  <li><strong>YouTube Shorts.</strong> The vertical, short-form section of YouTube.</li>
  <li><strong>Facebook Stories and Reels.</strong> The same format as Instagram.</li>
  <li><strong>WhatsApp status.</strong> If you use WhatsApp to talk to guests, a short vertical clip in your status is seen by your contacts.</li>
</ul>
<p>The 9:16 version also works well when you send a video directly to someone on their phone, for example in reply to a booking enquiry.</p>

<h2>Do platforms accept the same files?</h2>
<p>Not always, and the rules change. Each platform sets its own limits for length, file size, format and aspect ratio, and these are updated from time to time. A full 60 to 90 second video may be fine in one place and too long for a Story in another, where you might post only part of it.</p>
<p>Before you upload, check the current help pages of the platform. If a platform does not accept video at all, you can usually still link to the video on your own website or YouTube from the listing description, if the platform allows links.</p>

<h2>Which file do you get?</h2>
<p>You get MP4 files, ready to upload. MP4 is the format most websites, platforms and phones accept. You do not need any software to use them: you upload the file as you would a photo.</p>

<h2>How should you use the video on your website?</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Autoplay muted.</strong> Browsers usually only allow a video to start by itself if the sound is off. Our videos have no recorded sound from the scene, so a muted hero video works naturally. If you add music yourself, assume most visitors will see it without sound.</li>
  <li><strong>Loop or stop.</strong> A hero video can loop quietly in the background, or play once and stop on a still frame.</li>
  <li><strong>Mind the file size.</strong> A large video file slows down your page, especially on mobile. Your web developer or website builder may compress it, or host it on YouTube or Vimeo and embed it.</li>
  <li><strong>Pick a good poster image.</strong> The first frame, or the still image shown before the video loads, is what visitors see first. A strong exterior or a bright main room works best.</li>
  <li><strong>Keep the photos.</strong> The video adds to your photo gallery; it does not replace it. Guests still like to look at photos at their own pace.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How should you use it on social media?</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Make the first seconds count.</strong> On social media people decide quickly whether to keep watching. A video that opens with the yacht on the water or the best view of the property gives them a reason to stay.</li>
  <li><strong>Add captions or a short text.</strong> Many people scroll with the sound off. A short line on screen, such as the yacht's name or the hotel's location, tells them what they are looking at. Most platforms let you add text when you post.</li>
  <li><strong>Say where to book.</strong> Put the link or the next step in the caption, so someone who likes what they see knows what to do.</li>
  <li><strong>Post more than once.</strong> One video can be posted as a Reel, a Story and later again with a different caption, for example at the start of the season.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Can you use the video in ads?</h2>
<p>Yes. Once paid, you can use the video without limits for your own marketing: your website, listings, social media and ads. You just cannot resell it as a standalone video. Ad platforms have their own rules on formats and length, so check them before you set up a campaign.</p>

<h2>Do you need a separate video for each place?</h2>
<p>No. One video in two formats covers the places above. You need a separate video when the content is different: a different yacht, a different room type, or new photos after a renovation. Each of those counts as one video. A single video costs €100, including VAT, and the price per video drops with volume, down to €50. See the <a href={pagePath(lang, 'pricing')}>pricing</a> page.</p>

